  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A dying cold front is approaching northern New York and \nVermont from Canada this afternoon, producing some showers out ahead \nof it. The forcing will decrease as it moves east through northern \nNew York, but enough instability is out there to allow the showers \nto continue into Vermont. A couple embedded thunderstorms are \npossible as well. While sufficient deep layer shear will be in place \nfor potentially strong storms, low instability, some dry air aloft, \na lack of well-defined surface boundary, and pre-existing \nclouds/showers will be working against it. Therefore, an isolated \nstrong to severe storm is not likely this afternoon, but cannot be \ncompletely ruled out. Any storms will fall apart as they try to push \nsoutheast this evening and southern areas should remain mostly dry. \nChances of lingering showers overnight are around 10-40%, mainly \nnear the international border. High pressure will build down from \nCanada to end the week and start the weekend, bringing cooler \ntemperatures and lower humidity. Lows tonight will be seasonable in \nthe 50s, then cooling late week into the 40s and 50s. Highs through \nSaturday will be seasonable, with temperatures rising into the 70s \nand low 80s each afternoon. Next chance of precipitation arrives \nlater Saturday afternoon onward.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A quasi-double barrel pattern will develop by late \nSaturday with competing pieces of energy, one over central-eastern \nOntario, and a coastal low in southern New England. Models depict a \ndeepening trough over the Great Lakes by Saturday with a coastal low \nriding along the coast of southern New England. The deepening of the \nGreat Lakes trough will help amplify our flow pattern aloft Saturday \nnight into Sunday, likely accelerating the coastal low \nnortheastward. A few showers will be possible on the periphery of \nthe coastal low Saturday night in southeastern Vermont, though \nmajority of Vermont will stay dry Saturday and Saturday night. In \nnorthern New York, the Great Lakes trough will lead to increasing \nshower chances late Saturday into Saturday night across the St. \nLawrence Valley and northwestern Adirondacks. Questions linger on \nhow much moisture will be sandwiched between the two systems closer \nto Sunday morning, as it pertains to precipitation chances in \nVermont. Currently there is a 35-50% chance of rain Sunday morning \nfor much of Vermont, though these values may increase or decrease \nwith how the trough amplification occurs. A more delayed \namplification could keep the coastal system from racing east with a \nmoisture interaction in the area between the system Sunday morning. \nA faster amplification will likely lead to less system interactions \nand perhaps a dry Sunday morning. Regardless, as the Great Lakes \ntrough translates east Sunday, everyone will see periods of rain, \nand perhaps some thunderstorms should enough clearing take place to \nallow instability to grow. Temperatures will start off on the warmer \nthan average side in the upper 70s to low 80s, though with the \napproach of clouds and showers, temperatures by Sunday will begin a \ngradual decrease back to seasonable levels.\n\nThe main piece of energy departs late Sunday, though cyclonic flow \nabout a trailing upper low over eastern Ontario will keep some \nshower chances around. Lingering showers will be focused over the \nhigher terrain Monday and Tuesday afternoon, mainly diurnally \ndriven. Sky cover Monday and Tuesday will favor more clouds then sun \nas a result of the scattered shower chances. Temperatures will \nremain on the seasonable to potentially slightly below seasonable \nlevels Monday and Tuesday with highs in the low to mid 70s. \nTemperatures will rebound by mid week with subtle ridging building \nover eastern New England.",
